# .zshenv — read by EVERY zsh (login, non-login, interactive, script).
#
# `typeset -U` lives here and NOT in .zprofile for one specific reason:
# .zprofile is only read by LOGIN shells. When it held the dedupe, typing `zsh`
# at a prompt produced a duplicated fpath that hashed differently from the one
# recorded in the compdump (4,823 chars vs 1,862). oh-my-zsh compares those
# hashes (oh-my-zsh.sh:120) and DELETES the compdump on mismatch — so the next
# login shell paid a full cold compinit, 213-480ms. Setting it here makes every
# shell agree on fpath, so the dump survives.
#
# Keeping `path` in the list also makes the two manual `path=("${(u)path[@]}")`
# cleanup passes that used to live at the end of .zshrc unnecessary.
typeset -U path PATH fpath cdpath manpath

# Non-interactive zsh does not read .zprofile or .zshrc, so this is the only
# startup boundary that can guarantee pager-safe captured commands. A human
# zero remains local for inspection but must not leak into child processes.
if [[ ! -o interactive || ! -t 1 ]]; then
  if [[ ${ZSH_AGENT_MODE:-} == 0 ]]; then
    typeset +x ZSH_AGENT_MODE
  fi
  export PAGER=cat
  export MANPAGER=cat
  export GIT_PAGER=cat
  export DELTA_PAGER=cat
  export LESS=-FRX
fi

# Completion search path, declared ABSOLUTELY and read by every zsh.
#
# Two separate reasons this is here and in this shape.
#
# 1. Not in .zprofile, because only login shells read that. oh-my-zsh writes the
#    whole fpath array into the compdump as an `#omz fpath: ...` line and
#    re-checks it with `grep -Fx` on every start (oh-my-zsh.sh:113-122); any
#    difference at all deletes the dump and costs a cold compinit. While
#    ~/.docker/completions and ~/.zfunc were declared in .zprofile, a non-login
#    interactive shell built a 26-entry array against a login shell's 29.
#
# 2. Absolute, not `fpath=(new $fpath)`, because `brew shellenv` in .zprofile
#    emits `export FPATH`. An exported FPATH is inherited by every child shell —
#    including tmux panes, which take it from the server's environment — and zsh
#    imports it into fpath BEFORE this file runs. Appending to that inherited
#    array produced a different, longer one than a clean start did, which is the
#    same compdump-thrash bug wearing a different hat. Naming the entries
#    outright makes a clean login shell, a non-login shell, a nested shell and a
#    tmux pane all agree. (`typeset +x FPATH` does not help: the import has
#    already happened by then.)
#
# The three /usr entries are zsh 5.9's own defaults, reproduced here because the
# assignment is absolute. The Homebrew prefix is Apple Silicon and hardcoded, as
# it already is for `brew shellenv` in .zprofile; this file cannot run `brew`
# without paying a fork in every shell, scripts included.
# Homebrew's entry must stay FIRST. `brew shellenv` in .zprofile re-inserts it
# with `fpath[1,0]=`, and on a unique array a slice insert MOVES an existing
# element to the front rather than duplicating it. While it was already first
# that re-insert was a no-op; put anything ahead of it and login shells get a
# different order from non-login ones, which is the compdump-thrash bug again.
# Measured: moving ~/.zfunc to the front broke the four-way agreement below.
fpath=(
  /opt/homebrew/share/zsh/site-functions
  "$HOME/.docker/completions"
  /usr/local/share/zsh/site-functions
  /usr/share/zsh/site-functions
  "/usr/share/zsh/$ZSH_VERSION/functions"
  "$HOME/.zfunc"
)

. "$HOME/.cargo/env"

# Claude env (API key, base URL, telemetry) lives OUTSIDE chezmoi in
# ~/.claude/env. Read by every zsh before claude launches; the file itself is
# ignore-listed in chezmoi so a chezmoi apply can never override its secrets.
[[ -r "$HOME/.claude/env" ]] && source "$HOME/.claude/env"
